menu "osynth Synthesizer"

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_BLE
        bool "Enable BLE control (mobile app)"
        default y
        help
            Custom GATT service with a binary TLV protocol for the companion
            mobile app. See docs/BLE_PROTOCOL.md.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        bool "Enable I2S output to an external DAC"
        default n
        help
            Routes audio to an external I2S DAC (e.g. PCM5102) for higher
            quality output. Pinout in docs/HARDWARE.md. Off by default.

    choice OSYNTH_I2S_FRONTEND
        prompt "I2S analogue front end"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default OSYNTH_FRONTEND_DISCRETE
        help
            What is on the other end of the I2S port. This picks the
            *analogue* hardware; the digital side (block format, sample rate,
            the render chain) is identical either way.

        config OSYNTH_FRONTEND_DISCRETE
            bool "Discrete: PCM5102A DAC, optional PCM1808 ADC"
            help
                Separate converter chips, each strapped by pins and needing
                no firmware at all. The best-sounding option — a PCM5102A is
                a ~112 dB DAC against the ES8388's ~96 — and the one to pick
                if the line output is what the instrument is judged on.

                Line input needs a second chip (PCM1808) and two more GPIOs.

        config OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
            bool "ES8388 codec (line in + line out on one chip)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
            help
                One chip for both directions, as found on the ESP32-A1S,
                LyraT and most "ESP32 audio kit" boards. Converter quality is
                a step below a discrete PCM5102A, but it integrates the ADC,
                a microphone preamp and a headphone driver, and it is what is
                already soldered to those boards.

                Unlike the discrete chips the ES8388 has no strapping pins
                worth the name: it comes up unconfigured and silent, and
                stays that way until the firmware writes its registers over
                I2C. That is what OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_* below is for. It also
                needs MCLK in slave mode, so enabling this turns the I2S port
                into the same 32-bit-slot / 64x fs / MCLK arrangement the
                line input uses, whether or not capture is enabled.

                The analogue input and output pin pairs are selectable below,
                because boards wire them differently.
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_USB_AUDIO_TAP
        bool "Also stream to USB while the I2S DAC plays"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC && (IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4)
        default y
        help
            Sends every rendered block to the external I2S DAC and the USB
            audio interface at once, instead of the I2S output replacing the
            USB one.

            The DAC keeps the clock: its DMA still paces the render loop, and
            the USB write is best-effort, so a host that stalls mid-stream
            cannot stall the audio task. The two clocks are independent and
            drift apart; the USB class driver absorbs that by varying the
            isochronous packet between 47, 48 and 49 frames, which costs no
            samples and no resampling. Its correction range is roughly
            +/-1875 ppm, against the tens of ppm two crystals actually
            differ by.

            Costs a 256-byte copy per block - well under 1% of the block
            budget, and the USB stack is already running for MIDI either way
            - plus the EP-IN FIFO (OSYNTH_USB_FIFO_MS in usb_descriptors.h).

            Turning this off restores the older either/or behaviour, where
            enabling the I2S DAC leaves the USB audio interface enumerated
            but silent.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        bool "Stereo line input (I2S capture on the DAC's port)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC && (IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4)
        default y if OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default n
        help
            Adds a stereo line input on the *same* I2S port as the output —
            a PCM1808 alongside the PCM5102A on a discrete front end, or the
            ADC half of the ES8388 on a codec one. The I2S controller on the
            S3 and P4 is full duplex: a TX and an RX channel allocated
            together share BCLK and WS, so the converter runs as a slave off
            the two clock lines the output already drives and the capture is
            sample-locked to the playback by construction. Nothing drifts and
            nothing is ever resampled.

            The cost on the wire is that BCLK doubles, from 32x to 64x the
            sample rate. The PCM1808 always emits 24 bits inside a 32-bit
            slot and cannot be told otherwise, so the port has to run 32-bit
            slots; the firmware still reads and writes 16-bit samples, taken
            from the top of each slot. (The ES8388 is told the word length
            over I2C and is happy either way, but runs the same arrangement
            so there is one configuration to reason about.) MCLK becomes a
            real pin for the same reason - both converters need a system
            clock (12.288 MHz = 256x fs) even in slave mode, where the
            PCM5102A never did.

            On an ES8388 build this defaults on, since the ADC is already in
            the chip and costs no extra hardware. Turning it off leaves the
            codec playing and powers its ADC down.

            `in.route` places the input in the render chain, and what it
            records follows from where it lands:

              off  silent
              mon  monitored dry, mixed after the looper's record tap, so it
                   is heard but never printed into a take
              fx   into the FX bus, so it is reverberated *and* recorded
              dry  after the FX bus, recorded without them

            `in.gain` (0..4) is a digital trim after the ADC; clipping in
            front of the ADC is analogue and no firmware gain undoes it,
            which is why the `in pk` meter in the heartbeat reads *before*
            this gain.

            Monitoring latency is one DMA buffer plus a block, about 8-9 ms.
            The whole feature costs roughly 0.7% of the block budget and 256
            bytes of buffer. Off by default, and a build with it off is
            byte-for-byte the output path that shipped before it existed.

            Not on the classic ESP32: there the MCLK output cannot go through
            the GPIO matrix (it is routed via esp_clock_output, which is
            restricted to the CLK_OUT/strapping pins), and that chip's I2S
            treats a duplex configuration mismatch as a hard error.

            Wiring, straps and levels: docs/HARDWARE.md.

    # All five I2S pins are configurable, not just the two the line input
    # added: ES8388 boards come with their pinout already soldered, and a
    # hardcoded BCLK/WS/DOUT triple would rule most of them out.
    config OSYNTH_I2S_BCLK_GPIO
        int "I2S BCLK GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default 27 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 17 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 16

    config OSYNTH_I2S_WS_GPIO
        int "I2S LRCK/WS GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default 32 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 18 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 17

    config OSYNTH_I2S_DOUT_GPIO
        int "I2S DOUT GPIO (ESP32 -> DAC data in)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC
        default 33 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 19 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 18
        help
            PCM5102A DIN, or ES8388 DSDIN.

    config OSYNTH_I2S_DIN_GPIO
        int "I2S DIN GPIO (ADC data out -> ESP32)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        default 20 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 15
        help
            PCM1808 DOUT, or ES8388 ASDOUT. On the S3, keep clear of
            GPIO33-37, which are the octal PSRAM the looper's buffers live in.

    config OSYNTH_I2S_MCLK_GPIO
        int "I2S MCLK GPIO (system clock -> converter)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN || OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default 16 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 14
        help
            Drives the converter's system clock at 256x the sample rate
            (12.288 MHz at 48 kHz) — PCM1808 SCKI, or ES8388 MCLK.
            Mandatory for both: neither is a PCM5102A, and neither can
            reconstruct a clock from BCLK alone. Without it a PCM1808 reads
            silence while the starve counter climbs, and an ES8388 does not
            play at all.

    # ---- Second input: an external digital MEMS microphone (S37) ----------
    #
    # A separate I2S controller, not a second DIN on the DAC's port: one port
    # has one RX channel and one DIN pin, and the line input already holds it.
    # The S3 has two controllers and the P4 three, so the second one is free on
    # both targets.
    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN
        bool "Microphone input (I2S MEMS mic on a second port)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_DAC && (IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4)
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default n
        help
            A second audio input, for a digital MEMS microphone of the
            INMP441 / ICS-43434 / SPH0645 class.

            On by default on the P4, off on the S3. The P4 has three I2S
            controllers against the S3's two, its carrier still has GPIO4 and
            GPIO5 free after the I2S block took everything else, and the port
            costs one pin at SHARE_CLOCKS.

            The P4 also needed a fight over the clock source that the S3 did
            not — one of the candidates aborts mid-init rather than returning
            an error, so the list of what may even be *asked for* there is
            narrower than the list of what exists. That is settled and
            automatic now; the boot log names the source that took. The full
            account is in kClockAttempts (source_mic.cpp) and in
            sdkconfig.defaults.esp32p4, and it is worth reading before
            changing anything about this port's clocking.

            Nothing is heard either way until `in.source` is moved to `mic`,
            which defaults to `line`. A build with this off is byte-for-byte
            the input path that shipped before it existed, and a build with it
            on but nothing wired reads a dead port that costs the output
            nothing.

            It shares the downstream chain with the line input — one
            `in.route`, one placement in the render chain. `in.source` picks
            which device feeds it: `line`, `mic`, or **`both`**, which mixes
            the two. Alongside it sits `in.micgain`, the microphone's own
            level trim, because a line source and a MEMS mic do not arrive
            anywhere near each other in level and one shared `in.gain` across
            the pair would be wrong for one of them whichever way it is set.
            Both parameters are registered only on a build that has both
            devices; with one input compiled in there is nothing to choose and
            `in.gain` already is the trim.

            Each device has its own smoothed gain, so changing source is never
            a hard cut: `line` to `mic` is a genuine crossfade, and `line` to
            `both` ramps the microphone in over a line input that never stops.

            **What `both` costs.** Each device is captured every block whether
            or not it is selected — about 0.7% of the block budget and 256
            bytes each. That is deliberate: a device that has just been
            selected then hands over *current* audio instead of whatever its
            DMA ring accumulated while nobody was reading it, and its meters
            stay live so a microphone can be seen working before anything is
            switched to it. Mixing costs one extra SIMD pass over the block
            while two devices are audible, and nothing while one is. The two
            are summed in float, never as int16, so nothing clips at the
            capture that the float bus and the master soft clipper do not
            already handle.

            **Why a second controller.** The line input is the RX half of the
            DAC's own port, which is what makes it sample-locked to playback
            by construction. That port has exactly one DIN pin and the line
            input has it, so a microphone on other copper needs its own
            controller. See OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS for how the second one is
            kept in step with the first.

            **A MEMS mic is mono.** It drives one slot of the frame — which
            one is strapped by its L/R pin — and leaves the other alone.
            OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT says which to read; the sample is duplicated to
            both channels on the way in, so everything downstream still sees
            the stereo block it expects. `in pk` then reads the same figure
            on both channels and `fold` matches them, which is what a mono
            source correctly looks like on those meters.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS
        bool "Mic shares the DAC's BCLK and WS"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN
        default y
        help
            Run the mic's controller as an I2S **slave** off the same two
            clock pins the output port already drives, instead of having it
            master a second pair of its own.

            Do this if you can. It costs one pin instead of three, and it
            makes the capture sample-locked to playback exactly the way the
            line input is: one BCLK, one WS, one frame boundary, so there is
            nothing to resample and nothing to drift. Two masters at the same
            nominal rate do not drift either — both dividers hang off the same
            PLL — but they sit at an arbitrary phase offset from each other,
            and that offset is a thing that can be got wrong.

            The GPIO matrix allows one output driver and any number of input
            taps on a pad, and the I2S driver only enables the input on a pin
            it takes as an input (i2s_gpio_check_and_set, esp_driver_i2s), so
            the slave reading those pins does not disturb the master driving
            them.

            **The slot width is then not yours to choose.** A slave has to
            decode the frame the master is emitting, so the mic runs whatever
            the output port runs: 32-bit slots on a build with MCLK (an
            ES8388 or a PCM1808 is present), 16-bit on a bare PCM5102A. Both
            work with a 24-bit MEMS mic, which is MSB-first — 32-bit slots
            take its top 16 bits with 8 bits of slot to spare, and 16-bit
            slots take the same top 16 while the mic talks on into a frame
            that has already moved on. Turn this off and the width becomes
            OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT_BITS below.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_DIN_GPIO
        int "Mic data GPIO (mic SD/DOUT -> ESP32)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN
        default 4
        help
            The microphone's data output. With OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS on,
            this is the only pin the feature needs.

            Free on the S3 defaults: 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 21, 38-44, 47, 48.
            GPIO33-37 are the octal PSRAM the looper's buffers live in,
            GPIO0/3/45/46 are strapping pins, GPIO19/20 are the USB PHY, and
            8-18 are the codec bus, the SD card and the I2S port.

            On the P4 carrier the header is only GPIO1-5, 20, 32, 33, 45-47,
            and 45/46/47 do not drive — see PINMAP.md.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_BCLK_GPIO
        int "Mic BCLK GPIO (mic SCK)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN && !OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS
        default 6
        help
            Only when the mic masters its own clocks. With
            OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS on, the mic reads the output port's BCLK
            pin and this is ignored.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_WS_GPIO
        int "Mic LRCK/WS GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN && !OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS
        default 7
        help
            Only when the mic masters its own clocks — see
            OSYNTH_MIC_BCLK_GPIO.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_MCLK_GPIO
        int "Mic MCLK GPIO (-1 for none)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN && !OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS
        default -1
        help
            System clock for the microphone's port, at 256x the sample rate
            (12.288 MHz at 48 kHz). -1 leaves the pin unused, which is right
            for every bare MEMS mic: an INMP441 and its relatives derive
            everything from BCLK and have no such pin.

            Set it only when what is on the other end is a **codec** rather
            than a microphone — an ES8311 or ES7210 on a board's on-board mic,
            say. Those cannot convert without a system clock, and the symptom
            of leaving it out is the one this whole feature keeps producing:
            full blocks, no starves, every sample zero.

            A codec needs more than this pin, though. It comes up unconfigured
            and silent and stays that way until its registers are written over
            I2C, and components/codec/ implements the ES8388 and nothing else.
            The boot-time I2C scan is how you find out which problem you have:
            0x18 answering means an ES8311 is there and needs a driver, not
            just a clock.

            Only when the mic masters its own clocks — with SHARE_CLOCKS on it
            reads the output port's pins, and that port drives its own MCLK
            already.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_CODEC_ES8311
        bool "The mic is an analogue one behind an ES8311 codec"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN && OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default n
        help
            For a board whose on-board microphone is an **analogue** element
            wired to an ES8311's preamp rather than a digital MEMS part on the
            I2S bus. The ESP32-P4 carrier this was written for is exactly that:
            its own config says `microphone_type: analog`, `mic_gain: 24DB`.

            That distinction decides whether firmware is needed at all. A
            digital mic starts talking the moment it sees a clock. An analogue
            one reaches nothing until the codec's ADC converts it, so the four
            I2S pins are necessary and not sufficient, and the symptom of
            leaving this off is the one every fault on this port produces:
            full blocks, no starves, every sample zero.

            **ADC only.** The codec's DAC is powered down and never touched,
            and audio output stays entirely with the ES8388 on the main port —
            different chip, different I2C address (0x18 against 0x10/0x11),
            different I2S controller. Enabling this changes nothing about the
            output path.

            Depends on the ES8388 front end because it borrows that driver's
            I2C bus: both chips sit on the same two wires and the bus may only
            be opened once. See components/codec/codec_priv.h.

            Run the port at OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT_BITS=16 with it — see the note
            there.

    config OSYNTH_ES8311_MIC_GAIN
        int "ES8311 analogue PGA gain step (0-7, 6 dB each)"
        depends on OSYNTH_MIC_CODEC_ES8311
        range 0 7
        default 4
        help
            The codec's analogue preamp, ahead of its ADC: 0..7 is
            0/6/12/18/24/30/36/42 dB. This is the gain that actually buys
            signal-to-noise, because it lifts the microphone before the
            conversion rather than multiplying the result afterwards.

            4 (24 dB) is what the P4 carrier's own config asks for, and an
            analogue electret needs most of it before the converter sees
            anything worth converting. `in.micgain` rides on top for the
            digital trim.

            Too high clips in the analogue domain and no firmware gain undoes
            it — watch the `mic` peak in the heartbeat, which reads before
            `in.micgain` and after this.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT_BITS
        int "Mic slot width in bits (16 or 32)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN && !OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS
        range 16 32
        default 32
        help
            Bits on the wire per slot when the mic masters its own clocks.
            32 for every MEMS mic worth buying: they emit 24 bits MSB-first
            inside a 32-bit slot and cannot be told otherwise, which is the
            same reason the PCM1808 forces 32-bit slots on the main port.

            Only 16 and 32 are accepted. Shared clocks ignore this and follow
            the output port — see OSYNTH_MIC_SHARE_CLOCKS.

    choice OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT
        prompt "Which slot the mic drives"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN
        default OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT_LEFT
        help
            A MEMS mic is strapped to one half of the frame by its L/R pin:
            tied low it drives the left slot, tied high the right. Read the
            wrong one and the input is silent while the mic is working
            perfectly, so this is the first thing to swap when a new mic
            reads nothing.

            An INMP441 breakout usually ships with L/R floating, which is
            *not* a defined state — tie it.

        config OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT_LEFT
            bool "Left (mic L/R pin tied low)"
        config OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT_RIGHT
            bool "Right (mic L/R pin tied high)"
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_MIC_STEREO
        bool "Two mics / a stereo capture, keep both slots"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN
        default n
        help
            Take both slots as a stereo pair instead of duplicating one to
            both channels. For a pair of MEMS mics strapped opposite ways on
            one bus, or an ES7210-class array. OSYNTH_MIC_SLOT is then
            ignored.

    config OSYNTH_MIC_SHIFT
        int "Mic digital gain, in bits (0-8)"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_MIC_IN
        range 0 8
        default 0
        help
            Left-shift applied as the 24-bit word is truncated to 16, so it
            costs nothing: each bit is +6 dB, and it is taken from precision
            the int16 pipeline was going to discard anyway. Saturating, so an
            over-shifted loud passage clamps instead of wrapping.

            A MEMS mic at conversational distance sits well below full scale
            — 2 or 3 here is a reasonable starting point, and `in.gain` (up
            to +12 dB) rides on top of it. Prefer this to `in.gain` for the
            standing offset: `in.gain` multiplies a number that has already
            been truncated, this one moves the truncation point.

            Watch the `in pk` figure in the heartbeat while you set it. It
            reads after this shift and before `in.gain`, which is exactly the
            point this knob controls.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_SDA_GPIO
        int "ES8388 I2C SDA GPIO (codec CDATA pin)"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default 7 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 8
        help
            Goes to the codec's CDATA pin (its control pins are named CCLK
            and CDATA, not SCL/SDA).

            The firmware enables the ESP32's internal pull-ups so a bare
            board has some chance of working, but they are around 45k and
            the ES8388 datasheet asks for **1k** pull-ups on both lines —
            roughly 45x stronger. Fit real 1k resistors to 3V3 on a chip you
            wired yourself.

            Ready-made codec boards fit their own, but not always to that
            figure: the M5Stack Module Audio (M144) carries 2.2k. That is
            enough on a stacked module and can be marginal on jumper wires,
            where the control lines run alongside MCLK and BCLK — a second
            2.2k pair to 3V3 brings the bus to ~1.1k, which is what the
            datasheet asked for in the first place.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_I2C_SCL_GPIO
        int "ES8388 I2C SCL GPIO (codec CCLK pin)"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default 8 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 9
        help
            Goes to the codec's CCLK pin. Same 1k pull-up as CDATA above;
            the datasheet also suggests an R-C low-pass on this line.

    choice OSYNTH_ES8388_INPUT
        prompt "ES8388 analogue input"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388 && OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        default OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE2
        help
            Which of the codec's analogue input pairs the ADC listens to.
            Boards differ, and there is no way to detect it: on the ESP32-A1S
            and LyraT, LIN1/RIN1 is the on-board microphone and LIN2/RIN2 is
            the line/aux jack, which is why LINE2 is the default here.

            **The M5Stack Module Audio (M144) needs DIFF**, and that is the
            answer worth knowing because the symptom of getting it wrong is
            not silence. Its input jack is wired differentially, so a
            single-ended selection reads one leg of the pair and recovers
            exactly half the amplitude — a clean, unwavering 6 dB down on that
            channel, which sounds like a stereo image stuck to one side rather
            than like a misconfiguration. Nothing in the level or the noise
            floor says "wrong mode", and trimming the imbalance in the PGA or
            the ADC volume papers over it while throwing away half the signal.

            The tell is that the imbalance is *exactly* 2:1 and stays there
            regardless of source material. Crosstalk wanders; a divider does
            not; one leg of a differential pair is precisely half. If a board
            shows that, try DIFF before reaching for a balance trim.

        config OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE2
            bool "LIN2 / RIN2 (line or aux jack on most boards)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_LINE1
            bool "LIN1 / RIN1 (on-board microphone on most boards)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_IN_DIFF
            bool "Differential (M5Stack Module Audio M144)"
            help
                Each channel taken as the difference across its input pair
                rather than one pin against ground. Only correct if the board
                is wired that way — and the M144 is.
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_M5_LINE_MIC
        bool "M5 Module Audio: open the LINE/MIC jack path"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388 && OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        default y
        help
            Register 0x00 on the M5Stack Module Audio's STM32 (I2C 0x33). It
            connects the LINE/MIC jack to the codec's LIN1 and switches an
            electret bias onto the jack with it.

            On by default, which is also the module's own power-up state. Turn
            it off to take that bias and its 3.3k off the jack contact — worth
            trying with a line-level source, which needs no bias and can be
            loaded by it. Nothing on any other board reads this: the write is
            skipped unless something answers at 0x33.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_M5_HP_OMTP
        bool "M5 Module Audio: OMTP headset wiring instead of CTIA"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default n
        help
            Register 0x10 on the same STM32, driving the analogue mux that
            swaps the headset jack's mic and ground contacts. CTIA (off, the
            default) is the common standard; M5's own examples set OMTP.

            It decides which of the two contacts reaches the shared MIC_MONO_IN
            node, and that node sits on LIN1 — so it can affect the *input* as
            well as a headset microphone.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_ADC_BALANCE
        int "ES8388 input balance (left minus right, 0.5 dB steps)"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388 && OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        range -48 48
        default 0
        help
            Corrects a channel imbalance in the board's analogue input wiring,
            by attenuating the louder side in the codec's per-channel ADC
            digital volume (ADCVOL_L/ADCVOL_R). Positive attenuates the right,
            negative the left, 0 leaves both at 0 dB.

            This is the safe place to do it and the one to reach for first.
            Those registers only ever attenuate, so nothing here can clip, and
            the step is 0.5 dB rather than the PGA's 3 dB — fine enough to
            actually match a pair rather than land near it. What it costs is a
            little resolution on the channel being turned down: 6 dB of
            attenuation is a bit of the 16-bit word, against a converter with
            far more range than a line input ever uses.

            Defaults to 0: a correction here is a last resort, not the first
            thing to reach for. If a board's two inputs are mismatched, find
            out why before trimming it — on the M5Stack Module Audio (M144) the
            candidates are the STM32-controlled switches above, both of which
            sit on LIN1 and neither of which costs anything to try.

            Measure it with a *mono* source, the same content in both channels,
            and read `in pk L/R` from the heartbeat: the ratio is the imbalance
            and program material cannot skew it. A ratio of 0.5 is 6 dB, i.e.
            12 steps.

    config OSYNTH_ES8388_PGA_BALANCE
        int "ES8388 input balance in the analogue PGA (3 dB steps)"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388 && OSYNTH_ENABLE_I2S_LINE_IN
        range -8 8
        default 0
        help
            The same correction as OSYNTH_ES8388_ADC_BALANCE but made in the
            analogue PGA ahead of the converter, by *raising* the quieter
            channel instead of lowering the louder one.

            Better in principle — gain applied before the ADC costs no
            resolution at all — and worse in practice, which is why it defaults
            to 0. The step is 3 dB, too coarse to match a pair closely, and
            over-correcting drives the quiet channel's PGA into clipping. That
            clipping is analogue and unrecoverable, and it sounds like it:
            harsh and metallic on peaks. It is also asymmetric, so the channel
            you were trying to help is the one that breaks up.

            Worth using only on a board where the input is genuinely too quiet
            *and* the imbalance is a clean multiple of 3 dB. Otherwise leave it
            at 0 and use the ADC balance above.

            Both can be set at once; they add.

            ADCCONTROL1 carries two independent 4-bit PGA codes, left in the
            high nibble and right in the low. The offset is applied then
            clamped to the hardware's 0..8, so the top steps of `in.pga` lose
            some of the correction — that range is for microphones anyway.

    choice OSYNTH_ES8388_OUTPUT
        prompt "ES8388 analogue output"
        depends on OSYNTH_FRONTEND_ES8388
        default OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT_BOTH
        help
            Which output pairs to power. Driving one you are not using costs
            a little current and nothing else, so "both" is the safe answer
            when you are not sure how the board is wired.

        config OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT_BOTH
            bool "Both pairs (LOUT1/ROUT1 and LOUT2/ROUT2)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT1
            bool "LOUT1 / ROUT1 only (headphone jack on most boards)"
        config OSYNTH_ES8388_OUT2
            bool "LOUT2 / ROUT2 only (speaker amp input on most boards)"
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_LOCAL_UI
        bool "Enable local UI (LCD + encoders + buttons)"
        default n
        help
            Local control surface for the synthesizer settings.
            Off by default; the component compiles to a stub.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_SERIAL_MIDI
        bool "Enable serial (UART/DIN) MIDI input"
        default n
        help
            Alternative MIDI input, mainly for the classic ESP32 which has
            no USB-OTG hardware.

    config OSYNTH_SERIAL_MIDI_RX_GPIO
        int "Serial MIDI RX GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_ENABLE_SERIAL_MIDI
        default 35 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32
        default 21 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 5
        help
            UART RX pin for DIN MIDI input (UART1, 31250 baud). Needs a
            proper MIDI input circuit (optocoupler, e.g. 6N138) — see
            docs/HARDWARE.md.

    config OSYNTH_LOOP_PERSIST
        bool "Persist looper tracks (save/load slots)"
        depends on SPIRAM
        default y
        help
            Adds loop.save / loop.load trigger params (0x0605/0x0606) that
            copy the S15 looper's track set between PSRAM and durable
            storage. Tracks are IMA-ADPCM (4:1 vs raw int16; mono sets,
            S19, halve that again) both live in PSRAM and in storage since
            S20, so saves and loads are straight copies. Storage backend
            and sizing below. Needs PSRAM, like the looper itself — so the
            S3 and P4, not the classic ESP32.

    choice OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE
        prompt "Looper track storage backend"
        depends on OSYNTH_LOOP_PERSIST
        default OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH

        config OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH
            bool "Internal flash (spare region, no extra hardware)"
            help
                Raw flash above the partition table (0x400000 up to the
                module's flash end — pick the module below). One slot.
                Flash writes stall XIP code fetches, so save/load require
                the looper transport to be stopped. (SPI_FLASH_AUTO_SUSPEND
                would soften the residual stalls, but leave it off: on
                unsupported chips it fails flash init and bootloops on
                IDF v6 — see the note in sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3.)

        config OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_SD
            bool "External SD card (SPI mode, FAT)"
            help
                SD card on the FSPI pins (configurable below), 8 slots,
                song-scale capacity, and no XIP interference — save/load
                work while the loop keeps playing. Wiring in
                docs/HARDWARE.md.
    endchoice

    choice OSYNTH_FLASH_MODEL
        prompt "Flash size on the module (sizes the loops region)"
        depends on OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_FLASH
        default OSYNTH_FLASH_8MB
        help
            The loops region runs from 0x400000 to the end of the chip, so
            this is the only thing that sets its size. Named after the flash
            size alone, not the module part number, because the same choice
            serves S3 modules (N8R8, N16R8) and P4 boards (N16R32) — the
            PSRAM half of those names is irrelevant here.

        config OSYNTH_FLASH_8MB
            bool "8 MB (4 MB loops region, ~87 s stereo ADPCM, 2x mono)"

        config OSYNTH_FLASH_16MB
            bool "16 MB (12 MB loops region, ~260 s stereo ADPCM, 2x mono)"
            help
                Also set Serial flasher config -> Flash size -> 16 MB so
                the image header matches the chip. The loops region is
                verified against the detected chip size at boot and
                trimmed (or disabled) with a warning if it doesn't fit.

        config OSYNTH_FLASH_32MB
            bool "32 MB (28 MB loops region, ~10 min stereo ADPCM, 2x mono)"
            help
                Same caveat as 16 MB: match Serial flasher config -> Flash
                size to the chip.
    endchoice

    config OSYNTH_DRUM_SD_KITS
        bool "Load drum kits from an SD card"
        depends on SPIRAM
        default n
        help
            Lets the drum bus (S22) load kits from /sd/osynth/kits, either
            as `<name>.okit` images built by tools/gen_drumkit.py or as
            folders of .wav one-shots (name a file "03_snare.wav" to pin it
            to a slot). Kits are read into PSRAM, so this needs a PSRAM
            board; the factory kit embedded in the firmware works without
            it. Shares the SD bus and mount point with the looper's SD
            backend when both are enabled — whichever gets there first
            mounts the card.

    config OSYNTH_SAMPLE_KITS
        int "Recordable sample kits"
        depends on SPIRAM
        range 0 8
        default 8
        help
            How many recordable kits sit alongside the factory kit (S44).
            Each has DRUM_SLOTS pads that can be sampled from the audio
            input or from the synth's own output, and all of them are
            resident at once so a pad can be recorded without first
            selecting the kit it belongs to.

            An empty kit costs nothing but its slot table (~3 KB of PSRAM),
            so this is a limit on how many kits the app offers, not a
            reservation of audio memory — that is OSYNTH_SAMPLE_POOL_KB
            below, and it is shared across every kit.

            0 disables recording entirely and leaves the factory kit as the
            only kit, which is what a build with no input hardware wants.

    config OSYNTH_SAMPLE_POOL_KB
        int "Sample pool (KB of PSRAM, shared by every kit)"
        depends on OSYNTH_SAMPLE_KITS > 0
        range 256 32768
        default 4096
        help
            Ceiling on the audio held by every recorded pad across every
            kit, plus whatever is loaded from storage at boot. Recorded
            samples are mono 16-bit at the render rate, so 4096 KB is about
            43 seconds in total — plenty for one-shots across 128 pads,
            and deliberately not enough to be treated as a looper.

            This is taken out of the same PSRAM pool the looper sizes itself
            from, and drums_init() runs before looper_init(), so raising it
            shortens the advertised loop time rather than causing a failure
            later. That is the intended trade and the log line at boot
            reports both.

    config OSYNTH_SAMPLE_MAX_SEC
        int "Longest single take (seconds)"
        depends on OSYNTH_SAMPLE_KITS > 0
        range 1 30
        default 8
        help
            Length of the staging buffer one take is captured into, and so
            the hard ceiling on a single pad. Allocated once, on the first
            time anything is armed, and then kept — a build whose owner
            never samples pays nothing for this.

            8 seconds is a little over four bars at 120 BPM, which is what
            makes the slicer useful; it costs 768 KB while it exists.

    # The SD bus is shared: either feature turning it on makes the pins
    # relevant, so the pin options hang off this rather than off one of them.
    # Sample kits persist to the same card, so they join the list.
    config OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        bool
        default y if OSYNTH_LOOP_STORE_SD || OSYNTH_DRUM_SD_KITS || OSYNTH_SAMPLE_KITS > 0

    config OSYNTH_SD_CS_GPIO
        int "SD card CS GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 39 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 10

    config OSYNTH_SD_SCK_GPIO
        int "SD card SCK GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 40 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 12

    config OSYNTH_SD_MOSI_GPIO
        int "SD card MOSI GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 41 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 11

    config OSYNTH_SD_MISO_GPIO
        int "SD card MISO GPIO"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 42 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 13

    config OSYNTH_SD_PWR_LDO_CHAN
        int "On-chip LDO channel powering the SD rail (0 = externally powered)"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 4 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default 0
        help
            ESP32-P4 only, and only when the socket is wired to the SDMMC
            slot-1 pads (GPIO39-44). That pad block and, on every P4 carrier
            seen so far, the card's own VDD sit on a rail the chip does not
            power by default: it comes from an on-chip LDO output, VO4 by
            convention. Until the channel is acquired nothing on that rail
            responds — SCK never leaves 0 V — and the mount fails with
            ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT, which is indistinguishable in the log from an
            empty slot. That is the whole failure, and no amount of pin
            configuration fixes it.

            Set to 0 when the socket has its own regulator, or when the card
            is on ordinary GPIOs routed through the matrix: acquiring a
            channel that feeds something else on the board is not free.

    config OSYNTH_SD_PWR_LDO_MV
        int "SD rail voltage (mV)"
        depends on OSYNTH_SD_BUS
        default 3300
        help
            Ignored when OSYNTH_SD_PWR_LDO_CHAN is 0.

            The channel is acquired *non-adjustable*, which is what lets the
            looper's SD backend and the SD drum kits each hold a reference to
            it — an adjustable channel may have exactly one owner, and those
            two bring the bus up independently of each other. Nothing is lost
            by fixing it: adjusting the rail is for negotiating 1.8 V UHS-I
            signalling, which SPI mode never does.

            3300 is the SD default and what IDF's own examples use. Some
            carrier firmware for these boards runs the rail at 2700 instead;
            both sit inside the card's 2.7-3.6 V range, so try that if a card
            enumerates unreliably at 3300.

    config OSYNTH_RENDER_IN_IRAM
        bool "Place the audio render path in IRAM"
        default y
        help
            Marks the per-block render code (engines, voice manager, FX
            bus, looper, audio-task conversion) IRAM_ATTR so flash-cache
            misses from BLE/Wi-Fi or filesystem traffic cannot add jitter
            to the audio deadline.

            It costs far more than the "20-30 KB" this help used to claim:
            measured on the P4 build with tools/s41_iram_census.py, the
            .iram1 sections it creates come to ~98 KB (fx 42 K, engines
            24 K, graph 15 K, looper 6 K, audio_io 4 K, synth_core 3 K,
            drums 3 K) — more than half of everything osynth puts in
            internal RAM. Some of that is collected away by --gc-sections,
            but the order of magnitude is right.

            Turning it off is therefore a big, blunt lever, and usually the
            wrong first one: it buys internal RAM by giving up exactly the
            jitter immunity that filesystem and radio traffic make matter.
            Prefer moving a *peripheral* out of IRAM whose driver osynth
            does not depend on for audio — see the internal-RAM block in
            sdkconfig.defaults.esp32p4, which pays for the SD stack that
            way.

            THE ONE EXCEPTION (S47): with CONFIG_SPIRAM_XIP_FROM_PSRAM on,
            the paragraph above no longer applies. XiP moves the firmware
            image off flash and into PSRAM, and on the P4 those are two
            separate MSPI buses — so the code this option un-marks falls
            back to PSRAM, not to flash, and a flash write cannot stall it.
            The immunity becomes a property of where the image lives rather
            than of IRAM_ATTR, and the attribute is redundant rather than
            sacrificed. That is the only configuration in which turning
            this off is the *right* first lever; without XiP it is still
            the last one.

            The failure this answers does not say "region iram0
            overflowed" on the P4. It says

              --enable-non-contiguous-regions discards section `.sdata...'
              Total discarded sections size is NNNN bytes

            naming a hundred sections from libraries that have nothing to
            do with the change. That is the out-of-internal-RAM message,
            and NNNN is how many bytes have to be found.

    config OSYNTH_SPLIT_RENDER
        bool "Render the chain as a two-core pipeline (S45)"
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default n
        help
            Splits the render chain into two pipeline stages on separate
            cores: a voice stage, and a bus stage carrying everything
            after it — the drum bus, the FX bus, the looper, the sampler
            tap and the metronome — alongside the sink. The voice stage
            renders block N+1 while the bus stage finishes block N, so the
            two overlap and the per-block budget roughly doubles.

            How much that is worth depends on how the load actually
            divides, and it is rarely even. Measured on a granular patch it
            was 66% voices against 16% for the whole bus, so the ceiling
            was nearer 1.5x than 2x — and a patch whose voices alone
            exceed one core is not helped at all, because voices are a
            single stage and cannot be split further.

            The voice stage is pinned to the empty core (core 1) and the
            bus stage to core 0, where every task this firmware pins
            already lives. That is not arbitrary: a stage that goes over
            budget never blocks, so it holds its core at 100% until the
            patch gets cheaper. On core 0 that starves the BLE host, the
            sequencer clock and the idle task with it; on core 1 it costs
            only an idle task with nothing else to do. See kVoiceCore in
            components/audio_io/audio_io.cpp.

            Costs one block (1.33 ms at the defaults) of extra output
            latency, and about 3 KB of internal RAM for the handover
            buffers. All of it lands in .bss, so none of it touches the
            P4's scarce sram_low region.

            P4 only, and that gate is real rather than cautious. The
            bus stage holds the sink, and therefore the hard deadline,
            while sharing core 0 with the radio. On the P4 the BLE
            controller lives on the companion chip (SYNTH_BLE_VIA_HOSTED),
            so that core sees only the transport driver's interrupts. On
            the S3 the on-die controller runs high-priority interrupts
            pinned to core 0 that no priority here can preempt, landing
            them on exactly the stage that must not miss a block. The S3
            keeps the single-core chain, which is why render_chain() in
            main.cpp is still the composition of the same two stage
            bodies.

            Turn it off to A/B a fault against the single-core chain
            without changing targets: the chain order is defined once, so
            both paths render the same thing in the same sequence.

    config OSYNTH_ENABLE_MODULAR
        bool "Enable the modular patch graph engine (S28)"
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        default n
        help
            Adds a fifth engine whose signal path is a node graph the app
            patches with cables, instead of a fixed chain. Costs an audio
            buffer pool in internal RAM (kMaxBufs x voices x block size x
            4 B — about 12 KB at the defaults) plus the render kernels in
            IRAM.

            Off by default on the classic ESP32: the buffer pool and the
            extra IRAM are affordable there, but a freely patched graph
            cannot be fused the way the four fixed engines are, and the
            headroom left after the FX bus does not reliably fit one. The
            fixed engines are unaffected either way.

    config OSYNTH_FX_GPL
        bool "Include the GPL-3 reverb algorithms (MVerb, DuskVerb)"
        default n
        help
            Adds two more choices to fx.rev.algo: MVerb (Martin Eastwood, a
            Dattorro figure-of-eight plate) and DuskVerb Plate (Dusk Audio, a
            Dattorro tank with a density cascade per loop).

            This is a licence switch, not a feature switch. osynth is MIT;
            both of these algorithms are GPL-3. Building them in makes the
            resulting firmware image a combined work you may only distribute
            under GPL-3 — which among other things means shipping the
            complete corresponding source of whatever you flashed. Leaving
            it off keeps the default image MIT and still gives you two
            reverbs: the original freeverb, and the MIT-licensed WetReverb.

            No sound already saved depends on this. The algorithm enum keeps
            its numbering either way; a patch asking for one of the two GPL
            algorithms on an MIT build clamps to WetReverb.

            Costs roughly 95 KB of the reverb's shared PSRAM pool (the pool
            is sized to the largest algorithm compiled in, not to their sum)
            plus about 6 KB of code.

    config OSYNTH_VOICES
        int "Polyphony (number of voices)"
        range 1 16
        default 8

    config OSYNTH_BLOCK_SIZE
        int "Audio block size (samples)"
        range 32 256
        default 64

    config OSYNTH_SAMPLE_RATE
        int "Sample rate (Hz)"
        default 48000
        help
            48000 matches the USB Audio (UAC2) streaming format.

    config OSYNTH_HEARTBEAT_MS
        int "Heartbeat log interval (ms, 0 = off)"
        range 0 600000
        default 5000
        help
            Period of the "alive | ..." status line. Set to 0 to silence
            it entirely.

            This is a diagnostic knob, not just cosmetics: the heartbeat
            is the only periodic event in the firmware slower than 500 ms,
            so if you hear a click at the same period as this log line,
            the logging path is the suspect and not the synthesis. Each
            line is a ~250-byte console write, and with the secondary
            USB-Serial-JTAG console enabled it also pokes a USB peripheral
            that shares the S3's USB PHY with the TinyUSB audio device.
            Change this to 30000 (or 0) and listen: if the clicks follow
            the new period, that is the cause; if they keep their old
            rhythm, look elsewhere.

endmenu
